# Refactor Brief — Swift + `llama.cpp` (Metal): single resident model, hot-swap LoRA, KV cache, embeddings, chat templates, modern sampling

1. build & link
   compile `llama.cpp` with Metal and expose the C headers to Swift (XCFramework or `LLAMA_METAL=1 make libllama`). add a tiny module map so Swift can `import Llama`. no Python.

2. boot model & context
   on app start: `llama_backend_init()`, then `llama_load_model_from_file()` once, then `llama_new_context_with_model()` for your main chat context (and another context with `embeddings=true` if you want embeddings). keep the model alive for the app lifetime. run on a background queue (UI must stay responsive) (๑•̀‿•́)و

3. chat template
   format prompts with `llama_chat_apply_template(model, messages_json_or_struct, add_bos=...)` so your role turns/system/user/assistant are correct for the specific GGUF. don’t hand-roll.

4. LoRA hot-swap (GGUF adapters)
   load adapters once and switch them on the existing context. minimal pattern:

```c
// load once (store pointers)
llama_adapter_lora* fx = llama_adapter_lora_init(model, "/path/adapter_fx.gguf");
llama_adapter_lora* ry = llama_adapter_lora_init(model, "/path/adapter_ry.gguf");

// activate / scale
llama_set_adapter_lora(ctx, fx, 0.85f);

// swap later
llama_clear_adapter_lora(ctx);
llama_set_adapter_lora(ctx, ry, 0.60f);
```

keep only one active per context at a time; no full model reload. if you keep multiple concurrent chats with different adapters, create multiple contexts that share the same loaded model.

5. KV cache / sessions
   after prefill (or whenever convenient) snapshot the context and persist. restore to skip re-prefill.

```c
size_t n = llama_get_state_size(ctx);
std::vector<uint8_t> blob(n);
llama_state_get_data(ctx, blob.data());     // save

llama_set_state_data(ctx, blob.data());     // restore
```

KV is valid only for the same model+active adapter; keep separate blobs per adapter if you need fast resume across personas.

6. generation loop & sampler chain
   use the built-in sampler chain; wire only what you need (temperature, top-k, top-p, **min-p**, repetition penalties). feed logits each step, sample a token, detokenize, stream.

```c
llama_sampler* smpl = llama_sampler_chain_init(llama_sampler_chain_default_params());
llama_sampler_chain_add(smpl, llama_sampler_init_top_k(40));
llama_sampler_chain_add(smpl, llama_sampler_init_top_p(0.90f, 1));
llama_sampler_chain_add(smpl, llama_sampler_init_min_p(0.05f, 1));
llama_sampler_chain_add(smpl, llama_sampler_init_temp(0.80f));
// optional: repetition / typical / grammar / logit-bias

// per step:
llama_sampler_apply(smpl, ctx, /*cur_p*/, /*cur_n_tokens*/);
int tok = llama_sampler_sample(smpl, ctx);
```

detokenize with `llama_token_to_piece()` and stream to UI. stop on EOS or your custom stop set.

7. embeddings
   for embedding calls, use a context created with `cparams.embeddings = true`, eval the tokenized text, then read:

```c
const float* vec = llama_get_embeddings(ctx);        // sequence embedding
// or per-token with _ith if you need pooling yourself
```

return `[Float]` to Swift; store in your vector index as usual.

8. Swift wrapper (shape)
   wrap the above C calls in a tiny shim for Swift ergonomics:

```swift
final class LlamaEngine {
  init(modelURL: URL, ctxTokens: Int)  // boot model+ctx (Metal)
  func applyLoRA(_ url: URL, scale: Float)
  func clearLoRA()
  func saveState() -> Data
  func loadState(_ data: Data)
  func applyChatTemplate(_ messages: [Message]) -> String
  func generate(stream: (String) -> Void)  // uses sampler chain
  func embed(_ text: String) -> [Float]
}
```

9. notes that matter
   keep one model in memory; many contexts are fine (they share weights). for adapter changes mid-conversation, either maintain separate contexts per adapter or save/restore KV specific to each adapter. quantized base GGUF + GGUF-LoRA works; ensure adapter matches the base arch and tokenizer. on macOS, Metal offload is automatic when built with ggml-metal. that’s it (＾▽＾)

